        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5947aaa7-ae10-5655-a4e9-da907fd1c9e9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: William Calley Jr., J. Robert Elliott\n- Events: My Lai Massacre, My Lai investigation\n- Organizations: United States House of Representatives\n- Roles: platoon leader in C Company, federal judge\n\nFacts:\n- In the aftermath of the My Lai Massacre, twenty-six soldiers were charged with criminal offenses.\n- Lieutenant William Calley Jr., a platoon leader in C Company, was the only charged soldier convicted in connection with the My Lai Massacre.\n- William Calley Jr. was found guilty of killing 22 villagers and was originally sentenced to life imprisonment.\n- William Calley Jr. served only three and a half years under house arrest.\n- After three and a half years of house arrest, William Calley Jr. was released, though he was not technically exonerated.\n- Federal judge J. Robert Elliott ruled that William Calley Jr.'s trial had been prejudiced by pre-trial publicity, denial of subpoenas for certain defense witnesses, refusal by the United States House of Representatives to release executive-session testimony from its My Lai investigation, and inadequate notice of the charges.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "026ec540-1794-5475-b0f5-477bf822524b",
        "text": "Page: My Lai Massacre. Sentence: Twenty-six soldiers were charged with criminal offenses, but only Lieutenant William Calley Jr., a platoon leader in C Company, was convicted.\nPage: My Lai Massacre. Sentence: Found guilty of killing 22 villagers, he was originally given a life sentence, but served only three and a half years under house arrest.\nPage: William Calley. Sentence: While not technically exonerated, after three and a half years of house arrest, Calley was released after a ruling by federal judge J. Robert Elliott who found that Calley's trial had been prejudiced by pre-trial publicity, denial of subpoenas of certain defense witnesses, refusal of the United States House of Representatives to release testimony taken in executive session of its My Lai investigation, and inadequate notice of the charges.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
